      <section id="what-is-coding">
<h2>What is coding?</h2>
<p>Coding is the act of writing instructions that a computer can follow. That is the whole idea, and everything else in this article is detail built on top of it. When you open a weather app, send a message, play a game, pay for a coffee with your phone or watch a video, a computer is following a long list of instructions that a person wrote. The person who wrote those instructions was coding.</p>
<p>The instructions are written in a programming language. A programming language is a set of words, symbols and rules that lets a human describe what should happen in a way a machine can act on. English is full of ambiguity. If you tell a friend "put the book on the table by the window," they can work out which table you mean from context, and if they cannot, they ask. A computer cannot ask. It does exactly what the instructions say, so the instructions have to be exact. Learning to code is largely the skill of saying what you want so precisely that nothing is left to guess.</p>
<p>You will hear several words used for roughly the same thing. <em>Coding</em>, <em>programming</em> and <em>software development</em> overlap heavily. Some people use "coding" for the act of typing instructions and "programming" for the wider job of planning, testing and maintaining a whole program. In everyday speech the difference rarely matters. A <em>program</em> is a finished set of instructions. <em>Code</em> is the text of those instructions. A <em>programmer</em>, <em>developer</em> or <em>software engineer</em> is a person who writes them.</p>

<h3>A first look at real code</h3>
<p>Here is a tiny program written in Python. It stores a name in a variable and then prints a greeting.</p>
<pre><code>name = "Maya"
print("Hello, " + name + "!")</code></pre>
<p>The first line creates a variable called <code>name</code> and puts the text "Maya" inside it. A variable is a labelled box that holds a value. The second line asks the computer to print a message, joining three pieces of text together. When the program runs, the screen shows <code>Hello, Maya!</code>. Change "Maya" to your own name and the output changes with it. That small loop of write, run, look and adjust is the heart of the craft, and it feels the same whether you are writing two lines or two million.</p>
<p>The same idea written in JavaScript looks slightly different.</p>
<pre><code>let name = "Maya";
console.log("Hello, " + name + "!");</code></pre>
<p>The shape is the same. The words differ. <code>let</code> replaces the bare assignment, <code>console.log</code> replaces <code>print</code>, and a semicolon ends each line. This is the first thing most beginners discover about programming languages: they are different spellings of a smaller set of shared ideas. Once you understand variables, decisions, repetition and functions in one language, you carry that understanding into every other language you meet.</p>

<h3>The four ideas almost every program uses</h3>
<p>Nearly every program, from a calculator to a video game, is assembled from a handful of building blocks.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Values and variables.</strong> Programs work with numbers, text, true and false values, and collections of these. Variables give those values names so you can use them again later.</li>
<li><strong>Decisions.</strong> An <code>if</code> statement lets a program choose. If the user is old enough, show the page. Otherwise, show a message.</li>
<li><strong>Repetition.</strong> A loop repeats instructions. Send the same reminder to every person on a list. Check every square on a board. Keep asking for a password until the right one arrives.</li>
<li><strong>Functions.</strong> A function is a named, reusable piece of code. You write the steps once, give them a name, and run them whenever you need them, often with different inputs.</li>
</ul>
<p>Here is a short example that uses all four ideas together, again in Python.</p>
<pre><code>def describe(score):
    if score &gt;= 50:
        return "pass"
    return "try again"

for score in [72, 38, 91]:
    print(score, describe(score))</code></pre>
<p>The function <code>describe</code> takes a score and decides which word to give back. The loop at the bottom runs the function three times, once for each score in the list. The output is three lines: 72 pass, 38 try again, and 91 pass. Four ideas, six lines, and a program that does something useful. Real programs are far larger, but they are made of the same bricks.</p>

<h3>Coding is not only for professional programmers</h3>
<p>Writing code used to be a specialist job. Today it is a general skill that appears in many places. A biologist writes a short script to count cells in thousands of images. An accountant writes a formula-driven spreadsheet that updates itself. A teacher builds a quiz for the class. A musician writes code that generates sound. A farmer sets up sensors that water a field when the soil is dry. None of these people think of themselves as software engineers, and all of them are coding.</p>
<p>It is also a creative activity. Beginners often expect code to feel cold and mechanical, and then they build their first small game or drawing program and discover it feels closer to building with blocks or sketching with a very obedient pencil. You start with a blank file and end with something that did not exist before. That feeling is a large part of why people keep going.</p>
</section>

<section id="how-computers-run-code">
<h2>How a computer actually runs your code</h2>
<p>To understand why programming languages exist, it helps to know what a computer does underneath. At the lowest level a computer is a very large collection of tiny switches. Each switch is either on or off. We write those two states as 1 and 0, which is why computers are said to work in binary. A single 1 or 0 is called a <em>bit</em>. Eight bits make a <em>byte</em>, which is enough to represent one letter of text or a small number between 0 and 255.</p>
<p>Everything a computer handles is stored as patterns of bits. The photo on your phone is a long pattern of bits describing the color of each tiny dot. A song is a pattern describing the shape of a sound wave. Your code is also a pattern of bits, and so is the program that reads it.</p>

<h3>The processor and its instructions</h3>
<p>The part of the computer that does the work is the processor, often called the CPU. A processor understands a fixed, small list of very simple instructions, such as add two numbers, copy a value from one place to another, compare two values, or jump to a different instruction. These instructions are encoded as patterns of bits and together they are called <em>machine code</em>. Every family of processors has its own machine code. The chip in a typical laptop and the chip in a typical phone speak different dialects.</p>
<p>A processor works in a repeating cycle. It fetches the next instruction from memory, works out what the instruction means, carries it out, and moves on to the next one. Modern processors do this billions of times per second. A single instruction does almost nothing on its own. The power comes from the sheer number of them and from the way clever programmers combine them.</p>
<p>Writing machine code by hand is miserable. Early programmers did it, using switches and punched cards, and they quickly looked for something better. The first improvement was <em>assembly language</em>, where each machine instruction gets a short readable name such as <code>ADD</code> or <code>MOV</code>. A small program called an assembler translates those names back into the bit patterns. Assembly is still used today for very specialized work, but it is slow to write and tied to one kind of processor.</p>

<h3>High-level languages and translation</h3>
<p>Higher-level languages go further. Instead of describing individual processor steps, you describe what you want in a form closer to human thinking, and a translation program works out the machine instructions. This is why you can write <code>print("Hello")</code> without knowing how a screen draws letters.</p>
<p>There are two main ways to do the translation, and most languages use one of them or a mix.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compiling.</strong> A compiler reads your whole program and produces a separate file of machine code. You then run that file. Because the translation happens ahead of time, the result often runs very fast. C, C++, Rust, Go and Fortran are usually compiled this way.</li>
<li><strong>Interpreting.</strong> An interpreter reads your program and carries out the instructions as it goes, without producing a standalone machine-code file first. This makes experimenting quick, because you can change a line and run it again immediately. Python, Ruby, Lua and PHP are typically run by interpreters.</li>
</ul>
<p>Real systems blur the line. Java and C# compile your code into an intermediate form called <em>bytecode</em>, which a virtual machine then runs. Modern JavaScript engines watch which parts of a program run most often and compile those parts to machine code while the program is running, a technique called just-in-time compilation. When people say a language "is compiled" or "is interpreted," they usually mean how its most common implementation works, not something built into the language itself. In principle you could write a compiler for Python or an interpreter for C, and people have.</p>

<h3>What happens when you press run</h3>
<p>Take the Python program from earlier. When you run it, the interpreter first reads the text and checks that it follows the language's grammar. This step is called parsing. If you forgot a bracket or misspelled a keyword, the interpreter stops here and reports a syntax error. If the text is valid, the interpreter turns it into an internal form it can execute and starts going through it line by line. It stores "Maya" in memory under the name <code>name</code>, builds the greeting by joining the pieces of text, and asks the operating system to display the result. The operating system, in turn, asks the screen hardware to light up the right pixels.</p>
<p>That chain is long. Your two lines sit on top of an interpreter, which sits on top of an operating system, which sits on top of firmware and hardware. Each layer hides complexity from the layer above it. This idea, called <em>abstraction</em>, is one of the most important in all of computing. It lets one person write a useful program without understanding every layer beneath it, and it is the reason a beginner can build something real in a weekend.</p>

<h3>Errors are part of the process</h3>
<p>Programs rarely work the first time. Mistakes in code are called <em>bugs</em>, and finding and fixing them is <em>debugging</em>. The word bug in engineering is older than computers, and a popular story credits a moth found in a Harvard computer relay in 1947 as the first "actual bug." The log entry about the moth is real, and its author joked about it because the term was already in use. Whatever the origin, every programmer meets bugs daily.</p>
<p>There are three broad kinds. <em>Syntax errors</em> break the grammar of the language, and the computer refuses to run the code. <em>Runtime errors</em> happen while the program is running, such as dividing by zero or opening a file that does not exist. <em>Logic errors</em> are the sneakiest. The program runs without complaint but does the wrong thing, such as adding when you meant to subtract. Beginners tend to treat error messages as scolding. Experienced programmers treat them as free hints, because a good error message usually names the file, the line and the problem. Learning to read them calmly is one of the quickest ways to improve.</p>
</section>

<section id="history">
<h2>A short history of coding</h2>
<p>Coding is older than electronic computers. The story starts with machines that followed instructions long before anyone called them programs.</p>

<h3>Before computers</h3>
<p>In 1804 Joseph Marie Jacquard demonstrated a loom that wove patterns by reading holes punched in a chain of cards. Change the cards and the loom wove a different design. This is an early example of a machine whose behavior is controlled by stored instructions rather than by its physical shape.</p>
<p>In the 1830s the English mathematician Charles Babbage designed the Analytical Engine, a mechanical general-purpose calculator that was never completed in his lifetime. Ada Lovelace, working from a description of the machine, wrote extensive notes in 1843. They included a step-by-step method for calculating a sequence of numbers known as Bernoulli numbers, which many historians regard as the first published computer program. She also saw something larger. She wrote that such a machine might work on more than numbers, and could in principle handle anything that could be expressed in symbols, such as music. It was a remarkable guess about a machine that did not yet exist.</p>

<h3>The first electronic programs</h3>
<p>In the 1940s the first large electronic computers appeared. They were programmed physically. The operators of ENIAC, which was completed in 1945, set switches and plugged cables to define each new task, which could take days. The idea that a program could be stored in the computer's memory next to its data, so that changing the program meant changing memory rather than rewiring, arrived in the later part of the decade and became the basis of nearly every computer since.</p>
<p>The first programming languages in the modern sense came soon after. Konrad Zuse designed a language called Plankalkül in the 1940s, though it was not widely known or implemented at the time. Assembly languages spread through the late 1940s and early 1950s. Then, in 1957, a team at IBM led by John Backus released <strong>Fortran</strong>, short for Formula Translation. It let scientists write mathematical formulas close to the way they wrote them on paper, and its compiler produced machine code efficient enough to compete with hand-written assembly. That result convinced the industry that high-level languages were practical. Fortran is still used for numerical computing today.</p>

<h3>The first wave of high-level languages</h3>
<p>The late 1950s produced a burst of languages that shaped everything after them.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lisp</strong> (1958), created by John McCarthy, treated code as data and introduced ideas such as recursion and garbage collection that later became common. It became a favorite language of early artificial intelligence research.</li>
<li><strong>COBOL</strong> (1959) was designed by a committee for business data processing, with Grace Hopper's earlier work on English-like languages as a strong influence. Its code reads almost like sentences. A great deal of banking and government software written in COBOL still runs today.</li>
<li><strong>ALGOL</strong> (1958 and 1960) was designed by an international group and never became a commercial hit, but its structure, with blocks, nested scopes and a formal grammar, shaped the design of Pascal, C and many descendants.</li>
</ul>
<p>In 1964 John Kemeny and Thomas Kurtz at Dartmouth College created <strong>BASIC</strong>, a language meant to be simple enough for students in any subject to use. BASIC later shipped on the first home computers and gave a generation their first taste of programming.</p>

<h3>Structure, objects and C</h3>
<p>By the late 1960s programs were growing large and tangled, and the profession worried about it. A famous 1968 letter by Edsger Dijkstra argued against the unrestricted "goto" jump, and the movement it fed is called <em>structured programming</em>: build programs from clear blocks, decisions and loops rather than arbitrary jumps. Niklaus Wirth's <strong>Pascal</strong> (1970) taught this style to a generation of students.</p>
<p>In the 1960s Ole-Johan Dahl and Kristen Nygaard created <strong>Simula</strong>, which introduced objects and classes. Alan Kay and his colleagues at Xerox PARC developed <strong>Smalltalk</strong> in the 1970s, taking the idea much further and giving it the name <em>object-oriented programming</em> in the form we know today. In this style a program is a society of objects, each holding its own data and its own behavior, that send messages to each other.</p>
<p>Meanwhile, at Bell Labs, Dennis Ritchie created <strong>C</strong> in the early 1970s while building the Unix operating system with Ken Thompson. C gave programmers direct control over memory while still being readable, and Unix was rewritten in it, which helped make Unix portable across different machines. C became enormously influential. Its syntax of curly braces, semicolons and <code>for</code> loops shows up in C++, Java, C#, JavaScript, Go, Rust, Swift and many more. A large share of the operating systems, databases and embedded devices in the world still contain C code.</p>
<p>The 1970s also produced <strong>SQL</strong>, a language for asking questions of databases, <strong>Prolog</strong>, a language in which you state facts and rules and let the system search for answers, <strong>ML</strong>, which introduced powerful type inference, and <strong>Scheme</strong>, a small and elegant descendant of Lisp. A tradition also began in 1978 with Brian Kernighan and Dennis Ritchie's book <em>The C Programming Language</em>, whose first example printed the words "hello, world." Printing a greeting as your first program is still a convention across nearly every language.</p>

<h3>The personal computer era</h3>
<p>The 1980s and 1990s brought computers into homes and offices, and languages followed. <strong>C++</strong>, created by Bjarne Stroustrup and first released in 1985, added classes and other features on top of C. <strong>Objective-C</strong> blended C with Smalltalk-style messaging and later powered Apple's platforms. <strong>Perl</strong> (1987), by Larry Wall, became the duct tape of early web and system scripting. <strong>Haskell</strong> (1990) was designed by a committee of researchers as a standard pure functional language.</p>
<p>Then came a wave in 1991 to 1995 that still defines everyday programming. <strong>Python</strong>, created by Guido van Rossum and released in 1991, aimed for code that reads clearly. <strong>Visual Basic</strong> made it easy to draw windows and attach code to buttons. In 1995 three languages appeared within a few months of each other. <strong>Java</strong>, from James Gosling's team at Sun Microsystems, promised "write once, run anywhere" using a virtual machine. <strong>JavaScript</strong>, written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in a famously short time, brought programming into the web browser. <strong>PHP</strong>, begun by Rasmus Lerdorf, and <strong>Ruby</strong>, by Yukihiro Matsumoto, also date from this period. The web had arrived, and it needed languages.</p>

<h3>The modern period</h3>
<p>Since 2000 the pattern has been new languages that fix pain points in older ones. <strong>C#</strong> (2000) arrived with Microsoft's .NET platform. <strong>Scala</strong> (2004) blended object-oriented and functional ideas on the Java platform. <strong>Go</strong> (2009), from Robert Griesemer, Rob Pike and Ken Thompson at Google, aimed for simplicity and fast builds for server software. <strong>Rust</strong>, started by Graydon Hoare and adopted by Mozilla, offered memory safety without a garbage collector, and reached its stable 1.0 release in 2015. <strong>TypeScript</strong> (2012), led by Anders Hejlsberg at Microsoft, added types to JavaScript. <strong>Kotlin</strong> (2011) became a popular, friendlier alternative to Java. <strong>Swift</strong> (2014) replaced Objective-C as Apple's main language. <strong>Julia</strong> (2012) targeted fast scientific computing, and newer entries such as Zig and Mojo keep the cycle going.</p>
<p>The trend is clear. Each generation of languages tries to keep what worked, remove what hurt and take advantage of faster hardware and better compilers. No language has been the final answer, and none of the old ones has fully died. That is why the question of how many languages exist has such a large and slippery answer, which we turn to after looking at what a language is made of.</p>
</section>

<section id="anatomy">
<h2>What a programming language is made of</h2>
<p>Programming languages look different on the surface, but they share a common anatomy. If you know the parts, any new language becomes easier to read, because you know what to look for.</p>

<h3>Syntax: the grammar</h3>
<p>Syntax is the set of rules for how code must be written. It decides whether you wrap a condition in brackets, whether lines end with a semicolon, whether blocks are marked with curly braces or indentation, and whether a keyword is spelled <code>def</code>, <code>function</code>, <code>fn</code> or <code>fun</code>. Syntax is the most visible difference between languages and also the shallowest. Python uses indentation to show which lines belong inside a loop. C and Java use curly braces. Lisp uses parentheses around everything. These are differences of spelling and punctuation.</p>
<p>Here is the same small task, doubling a number, written in several languages. Notice how little the underlying idea changes.</p>
<pre><code># Python
def double(x):
    return x * 2

// JavaScript
function double(x) {
  return x * 2;
}

// Rust
fn double(x: i32) -&gt; i32 {
    x * 2
}

;; Clojure
(defn double-it [x] (* x 2))</code></pre>
<p>Each version defines a named function, accepts one input and returns that input times two. Rust adds type information. Clojure puts the operator first. The meaning is identical.</p>

<h3>Semantics: the meaning</h3>
<p>Semantics describes what the code means and what it does when it runs. Two languages can look alike and behave differently. In C and Java, <code>7 / 2</code> on two whole numbers gives 3, because integer division throws away the remainder. In Python 3 and JavaScript the same expression gives 3.5, and in Python you write <code>7 // 2</code> to get 3. Equality, copying, how functions receive their inputs and what happens when you go past the end of a list all follow rules that vary from language to language. Learning a new language is mostly about learning its semantics, because the syntax is quick to pick up.</p>

<h3>Data types</h3>
<p>Values come in kinds. The common ones are whole numbers, decimal numbers, text (called strings), true and false values (called Booleans), and collections such as lists, sets and dictionaries that hold many values together. A language's type rules say what you can do with each kind. You can add two numbers or join two strings. Adding a number to a string is either an error, a surprising conversion or a deliberate feature, depending on the language. The choice of type rules has a huge effect on how a language feels, and we return to it in the section on typing.</p>

<h3>Control flow</h3>
<p>Control flow is how a language decides what runs next. It includes conditions (<code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, <code>switch</code> or <code>match</code>), loops (<code>for</code>, <code>while</code>), and ways to leave early (<code>break</code>, <code>return</code>). It also includes error handling, such as <code>try</code> and <code>catch</code> blocks in many languages or explicit result values in others. Control flow is the skeleton of a program. Once you can read it, you can follow what a program is going to do without running it.</p>

<h3>Functions and organization</h3>
<p>As programs grow, they need structure. Functions break work into named pieces. Modules and packages group related functions into files and libraries. Classes, in object-oriented languages, bundle data with the functions that work on it. The vocabulary changes, but every serious language has some way to split a big problem into smaller ones and reuse the pieces.</p>

<h3>The standard library and ecosystem</h3>
<p>A language is more than its grammar. Almost all of the practical power of a language comes from its <em>standard library</em>, the collection of ready-made tools that ships with it, and from its <em>ecosystem</em> of third-party packages. Python's reputation in data science comes mostly from libraries such as NumPy and pandas rather than from anything special in the core syntax. JavaScript's reach in web development comes from the fact that browsers run it, plus an enormous collection of packages. When people pick a language for a project, they are often picking its libraries, its tools and its community as much as its grammar.</p>
</section>
<section id="how-many">
<h2>How many programming languages are there?</h2>
<p>Nobody knows the exact number, and the reason is interesting. The answer depends on what you decide to count, and people disagree about the rules.</p>
<p>Different catalogues give very different totals. A curated list of notable languages on a large online encyclopedia runs to several hundred entries. A much more ambitious historical catalogue, the Online Historical Encyclopaedia of Programming Languages, records more than eight thousand languages from the 1940s onward. Both numbers are reasonable. They are answering different questions. The honest summary is that the total sits somewhere between a few hundred and well into the thousands, depending on how generous the counting is.</p>

<h3>Why the count is slippery</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dialects and versions.</strong> Is Python 2 a different language from Python 3? They differ enough to break each other's programs, and the community treated them as separate for years. Is C++20 a different language from C++98? Strictly they are versions of one language, but many catalogues list dialects separately.</li>
<li><strong>Domain-specific languages.</strong> Many languages exist to do one job well. SQL queries databases. Regular expressions describe text patterns. Verilog and VHDL describe electronic circuits. Shader languages describe how graphics chips color pixels. Whether such tools count as programming languages depends on who is asked.</li>
<li><strong>Markup and style languages.</strong> HTML describes the structure of a web page and CSS describes its appearance. Neither one executes steps the way Python does, so some people say they are not programming languages in the strict sense. Others point out that CSS can now do calculations and animations. This app includes both because most people who learn to code meet them early, and the line between the camps is blurrier than it first appears.</li>
<li><strong>Experimental and personal languages.</strong> Students, researchers and hobbyists invent languages constantly. Most never leave a single laptop. Some reach a small audience, and a very few become widely used. Whether an abandoned experiment from 1987 deserves a row in a catalogue is a judgment call.</li>
<li><strong>Esoteric languages.</strong> Some languages are built as puzzles or jokes. Brainfuck, from 1993, has only eight commands and is almost impossible to read. Whitespace uses only spaces, tabs and line breaks. LOLCODE writes programs in internet cat-speak. Piet programs are abstract images. They are real languages with real compilers, and they inflate the count.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How many matter in practice?</h3>
<p>The number of languages people use for real work is much smaller. Industry rankings such as the TIOBE index, the Stack Overflow Developer Survey and GitHub's yearly reports all measure popularity in different ways, and they agree on a general picture. A small group sits at the top and carries most of the world's new code: Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, Java, C#, C++, C, Go, Rust, PHP, Swift and Kotlin, along with SQL for databases. Behind them is a long tail of several dozen well-supported languages with active communities, and behind that a very long tail of niche and historical ones.</p>
<p>That shape is why this app teaches fifty. Fifty covers the top of the ranking, the languages that define major platforms, the main teaching languages, the major functional and scripting languages, and a handful of historically important ones. If you finish fifty beginner paths, you will have seen nearly every major idea in programming, written with every major style of punctuation.</p>
<p>It is also reassuring to know that you do not need to learn them all. Professionals typically use a few at work and can pick up another in weeks, because the shared concepts transfer. The number of languages is large mainly because the number of problems people want to solve is large.</p>
</section>

<section id="paradigms">
<h2>The main families of languages</h2>
<p>Languages can be grouped by the style of thinking they encourage. These styles are called <em>paradigms</em>. Most modern languages support more than one, but each has a home territory.</p>

<h3>Imperative and procedural</h3>
<p>Imperative code is a sequence of commands that change the state of the program. Procedural code organizes those commands into procedures, which are what other languages call functions. C, Pascal, Fortran, COBOL and Go are largely in this tradition. The mental model is a recipe: do this, then this, then repeat that until a condition is met.</p>

<h3>Object-oriented</h3>
<p>Object-oriented languages organize a program around objects that bundle data with the operations on that data. A <code>Player</code> object might hold a name and a score and know how to add points to itself. Classes describe what kinds of objects exist. Java, C#, C++, Swift, Kotlin, Ruby, Smalltalk and Objective-C are strongly object-oriented, and Python and JavaScript support the style too. This model is popular for large programs because it lets teams divide a system into parts that each look after their own data.</p>

<h3>Functional</h3>
<p>Functional languages treat computation as the evaluation of functions and discourage changing values after they are created. Instead of updating a variable in a loop, you transform one collection into another. Haskell, OCaml, F#, Clojure, Elixir, Erlang, Scheme, Racket, Lisp and Elm are in this family, and many mainstream languages have borrowed its ideas. Supporters value that code with no hidden changes is easier to reason about and easier to run on many processors at once.</p>
<p>The difference is easiest to see in a small example. Suppose you want the sum of the squares of the even numbers in a list. Here is an imperative version in Python.</p>
<pre><code>total = 0
for n in numbers:
    if n % 2 == 0:
        total = total + n * n</code></pre>
<p>And here is a functional-style version of the same job.</p>
<pre><code>total = sum(n * n for n in numbers if n % 2 == 0)</code></pre>
<p>Both give the same answer. The first describes how to do it step by step, changing <code>total</code> as it goes. The second describes what the answer is. Neither is better in every situation, and being able to read both is a real advantage.</p>

<h3>Logic and declarative</h3>
<p>In declarative languages you describe the result you want and let the system work out how to produce it. SQL is the most widely used example. You write which rows you want and the database decides how to find them. Prolog goes further: you state facts and rules, and the system searches for answers that satisfy them. HTML and CSS are declarative too, since you describe a page and the browser draws it.</p>

<h3>Concurrent and event-driven</h3>
<p>Some programs must do many things at once, such as a chat server handling thousands of people or a game responding to the keyboard while animating a scene. Languages have different answers. Erlang and Elixir run vast numbers of tiny isolated processes that communicate by sending messages. Go offers lightweight tasks called goroutines. JavaScript uses an event loop and async functions. Rust uses its ownership rules to prevent a class of data races at compile time. If you build networked software, this part of the language matters a great deal.</p>

<h3>Multi-paradigm languages</h3>
<p>Most languages people use today are multi-paradigm. Python, JavaScript, Scala, Kotlin, Swift, Rust and C++ all let you write in more than one style. This is convenient, and it has a cost: two programmers can write the same program in very different styles. Style guides and team conventions exist partly to keep this under control.</p>
</section>

<section id="typing-and-memory">
<h2>Typing, memory and other design choices</h2>
<p>Beyond paradigm, languages differ in a few deep design decisions. These decisions shape how a language feels day to day and what kinds of mistakes it catches for you.</p>

<h3>Static and dynamic typing</h3>
<p>In a <em>statically typed</em> language, the type of each value is checked before the program runs, usually when it is compiled. If you try to add a number to a list, the compiler stops you before you can run the program. Java, C, C++, C#, Go, Rust, Swift, Kotlin, Haskell and many others work this way. In a <em>dynamically typed</em> language, types are checked while the program is running. Python, JavaScript, Ruby, Lua, Perl and PHP follow this approach. You can write less up front and experiment quickly, but some mistakes only show up when that exact line runs.</p>
<p>Static typing catches a lot of errors early and gives editors enough information to offer precise suggestions. Dynamic typing keeps small programs short and flexible. The two camps have moved closer together. Many statically typed languages now infer types so you rarely have to write them out. Dynamic languages have gained optional type annotations: Python has type hints, and TypeScript was created specifically to add a type checker on top of JavaScript. This blended approach is called gradual typing.</p>
<p>Do not confuse static with <em>strong</em>. A strongly typed language refuses to mix types silently. A weakly typed language converts between them freely, sometimes with surprising results. Python is dynamic and strong. Early C is static and relatively weak about some conversions. These are separate axes.</p>

<h3>Managing memory</h3>
<p>Every program needs memory for its data, and memory that is no longer needed must be given back. There are four common strategies.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Manual.</strong> In C and, to a large degree, C++, the programmer asks for memory and frees it. This gives the most control and the most ways to make mistakes, such as forgetting to free memory or using it after it was freed.</li>
<li><strong>Garbage collection.</strong> Java, C#, Go, Python, JavaScript and many others run a background process that finds unused data and reclaims it. Programmers get safety and convenience, and pay with some runtime cost and occasional pauses.</li>
<li><strong>Reference counting.</strong> Swift and Objective-C count how many places refer to each object and free it when the count reaches zero. This is predictable, though cycles of objects that refer to each other need careful handling.</li>
<li><strong>Ownership.</strong> Rust tracks, at compile time, which part of the program owns each piece of data. The compiler rejects programs that could use freed memory, so no garbage collector is needed. The price is a steeper learning curve.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Other trade-offs</h3>
<p>Languages also differ in how quickly programs start and run, how big their runtime is, how well they cooperate with other languages, how tooling and packages are managed, and how stable they are over time. COBOL and Fortran programs from decades ago can still be compiled today, which companies value. Some newer ecosystems change quickly, which brings improvements and also churn. None of these trade-offs has a universally correct answer. They are why a language that is excellent for a phone app can be a poor choice for a satellite.</p>
</section>

<section id="fifty-languages">
<h2>The fifty languages in Grelik</h2>
<p>The Grelik app covers fifty languages, and each one has its own set of quizzes written for its own syntax. It helps to see them in groups, because languages in the same group often share ideas.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The web.</strong> JavaScript, TypeScript, HTML, CSS, PHP, Ruby, Elm and Dart. Browsers run JavaScript directly, and TypeScript compiles into it. HTML and CSS describe pages. PHP and Ruby are common on the server side, Elm is a small language for front-end applications, and Dart powers the Flutter toolkit.</li>
<li><strong>Phones and desktop apps.</strong> Swift, Objective-C, Kotlin, Java, C#, Visual Basic and Dart. Swift and Objective-C belong to Apple's platforms, Kotlin and Java to Android, and C# and Visual Basic to Windows and the .NET family.</li>
<li><strong>Systems and performance.</strong> C, C++, Rust, Go, Zig, D, Ada, Nim, Crystal and Fortran. These are used for operating systems, game engines, network services, embedded devices and heavy numerical work. Ada is a long-standing choice for software where failure is not acceptable, such as aircraft and railway signaling.</li>
<li><strong>Data, science and AI.</strong> Python, R, MATLAB, Julia, SQL and Mojo. Python dominates machine learning. R is built around statistics. MATLAB is a staple of engineering education. Julia aims to be easy and fast at once. SQL talks to databases, and Mojo is a young language that aims to bring Python-like syntax to fast AI code.</li>
<li><strong>Functional languages.</strong> Haskell, OCaml, F#, Clojure, Scala, Elixir, Erlang, Lisp, Scheme and Racket. This group treats functions as the main building block. Erlang and Elixir are known for reliable, always-on systems such as telecom switches and messaging platforms.</li>
<li><strong>Scripting and automation.</strong> Bash, PowerShell, Perl, Lua, Tcl and Groovy. They glue programs together, automate chores, and embed inside larger applications. Lua, for example, is widely used to script games.</li>
<li><strong>Teaching and history.</strong> COBOL, Pascal, Smalltalk and Prolog. Each one represents an important idea: business-readable code, structured teaching, pure objects, and logic programming. COBOL is also very much alive inside banks and government systems.</li>
</ul>
<p>A reminder that the quizzes use the common way of writing each idea. Real projects often follow house styles, and some languages allow several valid spellings of the same thing. When the app shows you one spelling as the answer, other spellings may also work in real life. The goal is to get the shape of each language into your fingers.</p>
